spec: specs/optics-outside.md (stage 4)

why: the DESCRIBE interpreter still has no consumer that reads it, and
  "generate OpenAPI" would be a lane with no caller — the trap this
  arc has recorded three times. There IS a consumer already in the
  tree. A probe path lives in THREE independent string literals:
  okay-ops serves `r.url == "/healthz"` and `"/readyz"`; okay-deploy's
  `Health` defaults to those two as unrelated literals, rendered into
  SIX targets (k8s probes, an AWS ALB health_check, Azure, GCP, a
  compose healthcheck); and okay-script's ScriptDeploy writes a third
  pair by hand.

  CORRECTION, after reading ScriptDeploy rather than grepping it (the
  first version of this claim said the three had already diverged):
  they have NOT. `readinessPath = "/healthz"` there is deliberate and
  explained in place — a Site is ready when it is live, because the
  pages compile before the port binds. The defect is not a present
  divergence but the ABSENCE OF ANYTHING THAT WOULD NOTICE ONE:
  rename an ops path in `Site` and the manifest keeps the old literal,
  with Kubernetes finding out first by restarting the pod.

  The strongest form of the argument is that okay-script already holds
  this principle for SETTINGS and simply never extended it to paths —
  its own comment says the settings "are DERIVED from the value the
  program itself reads ... a name this deployment could invent does
  not exist".

what: Ops.routes and Site.opsRoutes become Routers. `Health` is built
  from a Router rather than from literals, so the assertion a test can
  finally make is: the probe path in the manifest is a path the router
  serves.

also (folded in, one line): record the okayCrdtNative lost-process
  recurrence in BACKLOG's native-runner-error entry, which the gate
  asked for at 23:12.

next: claim, spec stage 4, Ops first, then Site, then Health
